      "id": 1,
      "text": "Loring Park was first called Central Park when Minneapolis bought the 30‑acre plot in 1883.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Loring_Park",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1890 the park was renamed after Charles Morgridge Loring, the first president of the Minneapolis Park Board.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Loring_Park",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The park’s first permanent building, a heated two‑story shelter, was donated by Loring and opened in 1906.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21,
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Loring_Park",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Loring Park was the first Minneapolis park to install electric lights, illuminating the pond for winter skating in 1884.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Loring_Park",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The 1971 “Berger Fountain,” a copy of Sydney’s El Alamein Fountain, was gifted by Parks Commissioner Ben Berger and installed in 1974–75.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Loring_Park",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The park’s statue of Norwegian composer Ole Bull reflects Minneapolis’s early 20th‑century cultural ties to Scandinavia.",
